Multi-Thesis Resolution: A Portfolio Architecture for Scientific Research, with Application to Proxy-Control Identifiability

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

This work develops Multi-Thesis Resolution (MTR), a definitional architecture for organizing scientific research around a problem-centric portfolio of structurally independent theses, together with a conditional mathematical case study on Proxy-Control Identifiability in linear Gaussian state-space models. The mathematical core establishes a one-parameter scaling symmetry S_\lambda acting on the model class when the process-noise variance is free; the collapse of this symmetry to \mathbb{Z}/2 when the observation coefficient is known; the trivial-group reduction when either the noise variance or the observation coefficient is fixed; a revised completeness result for the identification set of the proxy coefficient b across all knowledge regimes, including the a=0 degeneracy and the \sigma_0-dependence; and a persistent-excitation condition sufficient for conditional identifiability when the observation coefficient is known. The structural-independence predicate is defined operationally for both mathematical and empirical theses, with a proof-checking predicate for mathematical failure events and a mixture-based correlation target. Algorithms for portfolio construction and refutation are specified. No empirical claim is made about the superiority of MTR over sequential research; the comparative protocol is specified but not executed.

Keywords: structural identifiability, scaling symmetry, proxy control, persistent excitation, observational equivalence, portfolio research methodology.

---

1. Scope and Epistemic Status

This work operates at three levels of claim, kept strictly separate.

(C1) Architectural claims. Statements about how research can be organized. These are definitional and methodological. They do not assert empirical superiority; they specify a structure.

(C2) Mathematical claims. Conditional propositions with explicit hypotheses. Their validity is relative to those hypotheses.

(C3) Empirical claims. Statements about comparative performance. None is asserted here.

The governing principle is that a proposition appearing convincing is not a reason to accept it. Every mathematical result is stated with its hypotheses explicit. No experiments, physical tests, or code training were conducted. The comparative protocol is specified but not executed.

