2. Mathematical Background

2.1 The Linear Gaussian State-Space Class

Consider the class \mathcal{M}_{\text{lin}} of models

X_{t+1} = aX_t + bU_t + \eta_t, \qquad 
Y_t = cX_t + dU_t + \epsilon_t,

with a,b,c,d\in\mathbb{R}, \eta_t\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_\eta^2), \epsilon_t\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_\epsilon^2), X_0\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_0^2), and \sigma_\eta,\sigma_\epsilon,\sigma_0>0. The target functional is \tau(M)=b, the proxy coefficient through which the control enters the state.

2.2 The Innovation Form and Observational Equivalence

The innovation form separates the deterministic input–output transfer function from the noise transfer function:

y(t) = G(q;\theta)u(t) + H(q;\theta)e(t),

G(q;\theta) = C(\theta)(qI - A(\theta))^{-1}B(\theta),
\qquad
H(q;\theta) = C(\theta)(qI - A(\theta))^{-1}K(\theta) + I,

where K(\theta) is the Kalman gain. For the scalar system, A=a, B=b, C=c, and

G(q) = \frac{cb}{q-a} + d.

The output noise spectrum is

S_Y(\omega) = \left|\frac{c}{e^{i\omega}-a}\right|^2\sigma_\eta^2 + \sigma_\epsilon^2.

Under rich excitation, observational equivalence is equality of G(q) and S_Y(\omega).

The steady-state Kalman gain satisfies the discrete algebraic Riccati equation (DARE):

P = a^2P + \sigma_\eta^2 - \frac{a^2c^2P^2}{c^2P+\sigma_\epsilon^2},
\qquad
K = \frac{acP}{c^2P+\sigma_\epsilon^2}.

The output spectrum already carries all identification-relevant information; the gain K is not needed for the identification set and is omitted from the main analysis.

Definition 2.1 (Observational equivalence). For a policy class \Pi,

M \sim_\Pi M' \iff \forall \pi\in\Pi,\ 
P_M^\pi(Y_{0:T},U_{0:T}) = P_{M'}^\pi(Y_{0:T},U_{0:T}).

Definition 2.2 (Identification set). For a functional \tau and observed laws \mathcal{P} = \{P^\pi : \pi\in\Pi_{\exp}\},

\mathcal{I}_\tau(\mathcal{P}) = \{\tau(M) : M\in\mathcal{M},\ P_M^\pi = P^\pi\ \forall \pi\in\Pi_{\exp}\}.

The identification set is indexed by the policy class actually experimented, \Pi_{\exp}, not merely by the knowledge regime.

2.3 The Scaling Symmetry

Proposition 2.1. In \mathcal{M}_{\text{lin}} with centered Gaussian noises and initial condition, for every \lambda\in\mathbb{R}^\ast the map

S_\lambda : (a,b,c,d,\sigma_\eta,\sigma_\epsilon,\sigma_0) \mapsto 
(a,\lambda b,c/\lambda,d,|\lambda|\sigma_\eta,\sigma_\epsilon,|\lambda|\sigma_0)

is an automorphism of \mathcal{M}_{\text{lin}} preserving P_M^\pi(Y_{0:T},U_{0:T}) for every \pi\in\Pi_{\text{closed}}. The family \{S_\lambda\} is a one-parameter group isomorphic to (\mathbb{R}^\ast,\cdot).

Proof. Set X'_t = \lambda X_t, \eta'_t = \lambda\eta_t. Then \eta'_t\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\lambda^2\sigma_\eta^2), so \sigma'_\eta = |\lambda|\sigma_\eta. The state equation becomes X'_{t+1} = aX'_t + (\lambda b)U_t + \eta'_t, giving b' = \lambda b with a unchanged. The observation equation becomes Y'_t = (c/\lambda)(\lambda X_t) + dU_t + \epsilon_t = Y_t, so c' = c/\lambda. The initial condition transforms as X'_0 = \lambda X_0 \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\lambda^2\sigma_0^2), giving \sigma'_0 = |\lambda|\sigma_0. The group property S_\lambda\circ S_\mu = S_{\lambda\mu} is immediate. \square

Corollary 2.1. The discrete sign-flip \Theta = S_{-1} sends (a,b,c,d,\sigma_\eta,\sigma_\epsilon,\sigma_0)\mapsto(a,-b,-c,d,\sigma_\eta,\sigma_\epsilon,\sigma_0).

Remark 2.1 (Normalization taxonomy). Fixing \sigma_\eta = \bar\sigma_\eta forces |\lambda|=1; the residual group is \{S_1,S_{-1}\}\cong\mathbb{Z}/2. Fixing c=c_0\neq 0 forces \lambda=1; the residual group is trivial. The two normalizations are not equivalent.

The scaling symmetry S_\lambda is a Lie symmetry of the linear Gaussian class. Lie symmetries of dynamical systems are transformations that leave the equations invariant, and their existence is tightly related to parameter non-identifiability.

2.4 Persistent Excitation

Definition 2.3. A deterministic input sequence \{U_t\}_{t=0}^{T-1} is persistently exciting of order n if

R_n = \frac{1}{T-n+1}\sum_{t=0}^{T-n}\varphi_t\varphi_t^\top,
\qquad
\varphi_t = (U_t,U_{t+1},\ldots,U_{t+n-1})^\top,

is positive definite. It can be shown that the identifiability of an nth-order linear process

G(q) = \frac{b_1q^{-1}+\cdots+b_nq^{-n}}{1+a_1q^{-1}+\cdots+a_nq^{-n}}

requires the test signal to be persistently exciting of order 2n.

For the model considered here, the transfer function d + cb/(q-a) has three parameters. However, a direct sufficient condition for identifying the product cb is the following: require one experiment in \Pi_{\exp} with U_0\neq 0 and U_1=0. Then

\mathbb{E}[Y_1] = c b U_0,

so g_1 = cb is identified directly. This condition is sufficient for identifying cb and is adopted as Assumption 3.1 below.

2.5 Relation to Discrete Symmetries

The distinction between continuous and discrete symmetries governs the type of non-identifiability. Discrete symmetries are the origin of parameters that are structurally locally identifiable but not globally (SLING); continuous symmetries represent an obstruction to local identifiability. In the linear Gaussian class, both types coexist when \sigma_\eta is free, and the continuous symmetry dominates. The collapse of S_\lambda to \mathbb{Z}/2 under fixed \sigma_\eta is precisely the passage from continuous to discrete obstruction.

