3. Revised Identification Set for the Proxy Coefficient

3.1 Standing Assumptions and Policy-Class Dependence

The identification set \mathcal{I}_b(\mathcal{P}) depends on \Pi_{\exp}. The analysis assumes:

Assumption 3.1 (Policy richness). \Pi_{\exp} contains an open-loop experiment with U_0\neq 0 and U_1=0, so that g_1 = cb is identifiable.

Assumption 3.2 (Generic parameters). a^\star\neq 0 except where explicitly stated; b^\star\neq 0 except in degenerate subcases; c^\star\neq 0 for cases (ii) and (iii).

Under Assumption 3.1, the invariants identifiable from \mathcal{P} are

d,\quad a,\quad cb,\quad c^2\sigma_\eta^2,\quad \sigma_\epsilon^2,

with the convention that when a=0, the noise term c\eta_{t-1}+\epsilon_t is white and the total variance c^2\sigma_\eta^2+\sigma_\epsilon^2 is identifiable, but c^2\sigma_\eta^2 and \sigma_\epsilon^2 are not separately.

3.2 The Revised Table

Case Regime Additional hypotheses \mathcal{I}_b(\mathcal{P})
(i) c=0 — \mathbb{R}
(ii) c unknown, \sigma_\eta free b^\star\neq 0, c^\star\neq 0, a^\star\neq 0 \mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}
(iii) c unknown, \sigma_\eta fixed b^\star\neq 0, c^\star\neq 0, a^\star\neq 0 \{-b^\star,b^\star\}
(iv) c=c_0\neq 0 known, \sigma_\eta free — \{b^\star\}
(v) c=c_0\neq 0 known, \sigma_\eta fixed — \{b^\star\}
(vi) b^\star=0, c^\star\neq 0, a^\star\neq 0 — \{0\}
(vii) b^\star=0, c^\star=0 — \mathbb{R}

Proposition 3.1. Under Assumptions 3.1 and 3.2, the identification set \mathcal{I}_b(\mathcal{P}) is characterized by the seven cases above.

Proof sketch.
(i) Y_t = dU_t + \epsilon_t; b does not enter the law.
(ii) Proposition 2.1 gives S_{b'/b^\star}(M^\star) observationally equivalent with target b' for every b'\neq 0. Since cb is identifiable and nonzero under Assumption 3.1, b'=0 is excluded.
(iii) With \sigma_\eta fixed, |\lambda|=1, so the orbit of b^\star is \{-b^\star,b^\star\}.
(iv)–(v) c=c_0 forces \lambda=1; the symmetry group is trivial. Proposition 3.3 gives b = g_1/c_0.
(vi) With b^\star=0, c^\star\neq 0, a^\star\neq 0, the output inherits the AR(1) autocorrelation of X. A candidate with b'\neq 0 introduces a policy-dependent component detectable under Assumption 3.1; c'=0 contradicts the AR component. Hence b'=0.
(vii) Y_t = dU_t + \epsilon_t; any M' with c'=0 produces the same law regardless of b'. \square

3.3 The a=0 Degeneracy

The table above assumes a^\star\neq 0 for cases (iii) and (vi). When a^\star=0, the noise term c\eta_{t-1}+\epsilon_t is white, and the following degeneracy occurs.

Proposition 3.2 (a=0, c unknown, \sigma_\eta fixed). Let a^\star=0, b^\star\neq 0, c^\star\neq 0, \sigma_\eta fixed to \bar\sigma_\eta, and let V^\star = c^{\star 2}\bar\sigma_\eta^2 + \sigma_\epsilon^{\star 2} denote the true total noise variance. Under Assumption 3.1, if \sigma_0 is free, the identification set is

\mathcal{I}_b(\mathcal{P}) = \left\{ b'\in\mathbb{R} : |b'| > \frac{|c^\star b^\star|\bar\sigma_\eta}{\sqrt{V^\star}} \right\}.

If \sigma_0 is known and \sigma_0\neq\sigma_\eta, then the difference V_0-V = c^2(\sigma_0^2-\sigma_\eta^2) pins |c| and the set collapses to \{\pm b^\star\}.

Proof. With a=0, the input–output law identifies d, cb, and V = c^2\bar\sigma_\eta^2+\sigma_\epsilon^2. For any b' with |b'| > |c^\star b^\star|\bar\sigma_\eta/\sqrt{V^\star}, set c' = c^\star b^\star/b' and \sigma_\epsilon'^2 = V - c'^2\bar\sigma_\eta^2. Then c'b' = c^\star b^\star and c'^2\bar\sigma_\eta^2+\sigma_\epsilon'^2 = V, so the law is reproduced. The strict inequality follows from \sigma_\epsilon>0. The \sigma_0-known branch follows from the additional invariant V_0. \square

Corollary 3.1 (a=0, case (vi)). If b^\star=0, c^\star\neq 0, a^\star=0, and c is unknown, then \mathcal{I}_b = \mathbb{R} when \sigma_0=\sigma_\eta: the model with c'=0 and adjusted \sigma_\epsilon' reproduces the white output law for any b'. If \sigma_0\neq\sigma_\eta, then \mathcal{I}_b = \{0\}.

3.4 Conditional Identifiability Under Known c

Proposition 3.3. Let \mathcal{M}_{\text{lin}}^{c_0} be \mathcal{M}_{\text{lin}} with c=c_0\in\mathbb{R}^\ast fixed and known. Let \Pi_{\text{exc}}\subseteq\Pi_{\text{open}} consist of policies whose induced inputs satisfy U_0\neq 0 and U_1=0. Then b is identifiable in \mathcal{M}_{\text{lin}}^{c_0} under \Pi_{\text{exc}}, regardless of whether \sigma_\eta is free or fixed.

Proof. With U_0\neq 0 and U_1=0, \mathbb{E}[Y_1] = c_0 b U_0, so g_1 = c_0 b is identifiable. Since c_0\neq 0 is known, b = g_1/c_0. The scaling symmetry S_\lambda sends c_0 to c_0/\lambda; the requirement c_0/\lambda = c_0 forces \lambda=1, so S_\lambda is broken entirely. \square

Proposition 3.4. Let \Pi\subseteq\Pi_{\text{open}}. For every \pi\in\Pi and every \lambda\in\mathbb{R}^\ast, M\sim_\Pi S_\lambda(M). Consequently, excitation alone cannot break S_\lambda, and b is not identifiable in \mathcal{M}_{\text{lin}} under any open-loop policy when c is unknown.

Proof. Immediate from Proposition 2.1, which holds for arbitrary \Pi\subseteq\Pi_{\text{closed}} and a fortiori for \Pi\subseteq\Pi_{\text{open}}. \square

3.5 Group-Orbit Theorem and Completeness

The case table is organized by the residual symmetry group G_K under knowledge regime K. The identification set is the orbit

\mathcal{I}_b = G_K \cdot b^\star.

The residual groups are:

· G = \mathbb{R}^\ast when c is unknown and \sigma_\eta is free.
· G = \mathbb{Z}/2 when c is unknown and \sigma_\eta is fixed.
· G = \{1\} when c is known.
· G = \mathbb{Z}/2 with sign constraint when \mathrm{sgn}(c) is known.

This group-orbit theorem covers sign-known and \sigma_0-known regimes that are absent from the table. With a^\star\neq 0 and Assumption 3.1, the seven cases plus the a=0 degeneracies partition the generic parameter space. The completeness claim is therefore conditional on generic parameters (a^\star\neq 0), rich policies (Assumption 3.1), and the listed knowledge regimes.

