4. The MTR Architecture

4.1 Thesis Portfolio Definition

Let P be a problem. A thesis portfolio is a tuple \mathcal{T} = (T_1,\ldots,T_n) where each T_i is a quadruple

T_i = (\mathcal{H}_i,\mathcal{A}_i,\mathcal{R}_i,\mathcal{D}_i)

with \mathcal{H}_i the central hypothesis, \mathcal{A}_i the set of assumptions, \mathcal{R}_i the expected result, and \mathcal{D}_i the set of dependencies.

The method of multiple working hypotheses is classical: Chamberlin (1890) advocated simultaneously evaluating several hypotheses, rejecting those that conflict with available data, so that the investigator becomes “the parent of a family of hypotheses” and is “forbidden to fasten his affections unduly upon any one”. Platt (1964) built on this with “strong inference,” devising alternative hypotheses and crucial experiments that exclude one or more of them.

4.2 Structural Independence: Operational Definition

Structural independence is defined by failure-mode correlation.

(a) Failure-scenario space. A finite or parameterized set \mathcal{S} = \{s_1,\ldots,s_m\}. For the identifiability problem, natural scenarios are noise-model misspecification, policy-class restriction, a\approx 1, c\approx 0, a=0, and small sample length T.

(b) Critical vs. auxiliary assumptions. An assumption a\in\mathcal{A}_i is critical if its failure invalidates T_i; auxiliary if T_i admits a repair. Independence requires that \mathcal{A}_i and \mathcal{A}_j share no critical undemonstrated assumption.

(c) Failure-correlation target. Define the mixture over scenarios with a pre-declared prior \pi(s):

\rho_{ij} = \Pr[T_i\text{ fails}\mid T_j\text{ fails}]
= \frac{\sum_{s\in\mathcal{S}}\pi(s)\Pr[T_i\text{ fails}\wedge T_j\text{ fails}\mid s]}
{\sum_{s\in\mathcal{S}}\pi(s)\Pr[T_j\text{ fails}\mid s]}.

Structural independence at threshold \epsilon holds when

|\rho_{ij} - \Pr[T_i\text{ fails}]| \le \epsilon.

The signed covariance or odds ratio should be reported alongside the conditional gap.

(d) Estimation procedure. Frozen before execution. Let N be pre-declared; the evaluator is blind to thesis identity. For each s\in\mathcal{S}, sample N parameter worlds \theta_1,\ldots,\theta_N from p(\theta\mid s); mark F_{i,k}\in\{\text{fail},\text{success}\}; estimate

\hat p_i(s) = \frac{1}{N}\sum_k\mathbf{1}[F_{i,k}=\text{fail}],
\qquad
\hat p_{ij}(s) = \frac{1}{N}\sum_k\mathbf{1}[F_{i,k}=\text{fail}\wedge F_{j,k}=\text{fail}],

and set \hat\rho_{ij} = \sum_s\pi(s)\hat p_{ij}(s)/\sum_s\pi(s)\hat p_j(s). A pilot with small N on deliberately dependent and deliberately independent theses calibrates \epsilon.

4.3 Failure Events for Mathematical Versus Empirical Theses

Empirical theses. Failure in parameter world \theta_k is a random draw: the thesis’s predicted observable consequence is compared to the simulated or experimental outcome.

Mathematical theses. Failure is a proof-checking outcome, not a random draw. Define a proof-checking predicate \mathcal{V}(T_i,\mathcal{L}) where \mathcal{L} is the logical framework. A mathematical thesis fails in scenario s if either the proof is invalid in \mathcal{L} under s, or a critical assumption of the thesis is violated by s. The failure-scenario space for mathematical theses is the set of assumption-violation scenarios, not a parameter prior.

The structural-independence predicate for mathematical theses reduces to: \mathcal{A}_i and \mathcal{A}_j share no critical undemonstrated assumption, and the proof of T_i does not depend on the proof of T_j. Conditioning on s alone would make the predicate vacuous, since given s each thesis either fails or does not. The mixture over s is therefore essential.

Shared generators—same LLM base model, same retrieval corpus, same human expert—are a source of dependence not captured by assumption disjointness alone. Recent work on correlated LLM errors shows that models agree 60% of the time when both err, and that larger and more accurate models have highly correlated errors even with distinct architectures. The Synthetic Consensus Cascade framework shows that when pairwise error correlation remains positive, increasing the number of agents does not drive majority-error probability to zero; instead, risk approaches a non-zero correlation-dependent floor. In a stress test with individual error probability p=0.08, nine agents, and error correlation \rho=0.25, the majority-wrong probability is 3.56\%, compared with 0.031\% under independence—a 113.6-fold difference. Structural independence must therefore account for shared generators explicitly.

The N-version programming literature provides a cautionary precedent. Eckhardt and Lee (1985) and Littlewood and Miller (1989) developed probabilistic models showing that “independently developed” software versions can fail coincidentally, and that the number of tests in which more than one program fails is often substantially greater than expected under independence. MTR’s independence predicate must be calibrated against such correlated-failure models, not merely asserted from assumption disjointness.

4.4 Five-Layer Architecture

1. Problem formalization. Explicit P, observations, objectives, constraints, known results, uncertainties, success criteria.
2. Decomposition. Dimensions derived from the structure of P.
3. Thesis generation. Hypothesis, mechanism, assumptions, expected result, validation method, failure conditions, refutation test.
4. Refutation engine. Counter-example search, hidden-assumption identification, prior-art comparison.
5. Status assignment. Validated, Partially validated, Conditional, Refuted, Redundant, Unresolved, Complementary.

4.5 Knowledge Graph Relations

Nodes are problems and solutions. Edges are:

\text{PROBLEM}\to\text{ADDRESSES}\to\text{THESIS},
\qquad
\text{THESIS}\to\text{DEPENDS\_ON}\to\text{ASSUMPTION},

\text{THESIS}\to\text{TESTED\_BY}\to\text{EXPERIMENT},
\qquad
\text{THESIS}_A\to\text{COMPLEMENTS}\to\text{THESIS}_B,

\text{FAILED\_TEST}\to\text{RESTRICTS}\to\text{VALIDITY\_DOMAIN}.

The final relation encodes that a failed test teaches the conditions under which the thesis fails.

4.6 Algorithm: Portfolio Construction and Refutation

```
Algorithm 1: MTR Portfolio Construction
Input: Problem P, budget B, threshold ε, prior π(s)
Output: Thesis portfolio T = (T_1,...,T_n) with statuses

1. Formalize P; declare S = {s_1,...,s_m} and π(s)
2. Decompose P into dimensions D_1,...,D_k
3. Generate candidate theses {T_i} with (H_i, A_i, R_i, D_i)
4. For each T_i:
     identify critical undemonstrated assumptions A_i^crit ⊆ A_i
5. For each pair (i,j):
     compute p̂_i(s), p̂_j(s), p̂_ij(s) for all s ∈ S
     compute ρ̂_ij = Σ_s π(s)p̂_ij(s) / Σ_s π(s)p̂_j(s)
     if |ρ̂_ij − p̂_i| > ε:
       mark T_i, T_j dependent; merge or discard
6. Allocate budget per §5.1
7. Execute refutation engine on each T_i
8. Assign status per §4.4
9. Return T with statuses and failure domains
```

Step 5 computes \hat\rho_{ij} explicitly and applies the threshold \epsilon, correcting the earlier inconsistency where calibration was mentioned but no step used it. Step 4 uses critical undemonstrated assumptions, consistent with §4.2(b). Problem-level givens such as “linear Gaussian class” and “\Pi_{\text{closed}}” are stored separately from thesis-level assumptions; otherwise every pair would be declared dependent.

