5. Pre-Registered Protocol for Comparative Evaluation

The following protocol is specified but not executed. It is included because the architectural claims of MTR are testable only against a pre-declared comparison.

5.1 Two Comparisons

Experiment A — Strict equal budget. Two strategies receive identical total budgets: sequential research (single hypothesis, iteratively revised) versus MTR (portfolio of structurally independent theses). A third arm is sequential research by a human expert. Budget split frozen before execution:

Category Fraction Contents
Generation 30% Problem decomposition, thesis enumeration
Verification 40% Proof checking, bibliographic audit
Numerical experimentation 20% Simulation, tool runs
Independent evaluation 10% Blind evaluator, inter-rater reliability

Experiment B — Cost-performance. Strategies may use different budgets; knowledge output per unit cost is measured. An overhead threshold is declared before observing results.

5.2 Verification Rule

A contribution counts as verified-reusable only under the following conditions:

· Theorem. Complete proof, checked line-by-line by a second person, no undemonstrated critical assumption. Generative-agent confirmation does not count.
· Lemma. Reusable intermediate step with proof and explicit hypotheses.
· Counter-example. Explicit parameter setting violating a stated assumption, with observable consequence.
· Domain restriction. Verified statement of the form “under conditions X, conclusion Y fails.”

Double counting is prohibited: a theorem that subsumes a lemma counts once, in the higher category.

5.3 Principal Criteria

Measured per unit of budget:

1. Verified-reusable yield. Number of verified results plus reusable partial results under §5.2.
2. Coverage. Number of distinct regions of a pre-declared taxonomy for which a verified conclusion is obtained. The taxonomy must be derived from external ground truth, not from the author’s own solution. For linear Gaussian identifiability, symbolic tools such as SIAN (Maple), STRIKE-GOLDD (MATLAB), and StructuralIdentifiability.jl (Julia) can compute ground-truth identification sets for a pre-declared family of models with varying a,b,c,d,\sigma_\eta,\sigma_\epsilon,\sigma_0. These tools are widely used and cross-validated.
3. Time to first verified conclusion.
4. Fraction of correct refutations. Ratio of verified refutations to total refutation attempts.

Raw number of refutations is a secondary diagnostic only.

5.4 Statistical Decision Rules

Each criterion is accompanied by a pre-declared effect-size measure, uncertainty interval, and multiple-comparison correction. The comparison is framed as a Bayesian decision problem with pre-declared priors and loss function.

5.5 Success and Refutation Conditions

· MTR success in Experiment A: superior on at least two of the four principal criteria under strict equal budget, with statistical significance as specified in §5.4.
· MTR refutation in Experiment A: not superior on at least two principal criteria.
· Threshold for Experiment B: maximum overhead fixed before execution.

5.6 Independent Evaluation

Final products are evaluated by an evaluator blind to strategy. Evaluation is budgeted separately. Inter-rater reliability for “verified-reusable yield” is estimated on a pre-declared subsample.

5.7 Limitations

· One problem, one protocol. Conclusions are confined to the identifiability problem and to this design. The n=1 problem means no methodological generalization is warranted. The protocol should be framed as a case study.
· LLM novelty judging. Correlated LLM errors mean that multiple agents agreeing on “this is novel” provides weak evidence. Novelty-status claims must be based on bibliographic comparison, not on LLM judgment.
· Fixed budget split. The 30/40/20/10 split is arbitrary. A more defensible design would justify it from a pilot or treat it as a design variable.

