6. Discussion

6.1 Status of the Mathematical Results

The mathematical core comprises one classical scaling symmetry, one classical non-identifiability consequence, one classical persistent-excitation result, and a revised seven-case packaging of the identification set with explicit policy-class dependence and separate treatment of the a=0 degeneracy. The packaging is the contribution: it makes explicit, for the proxy-control coefficient, the precise sense in which free process-noise scale destroys identifiability, and it separates the discrete from the continuous obstruction. The group-orbit theorem \mathcal{I}_b = G_K\cdot b^\star unifies the cases and covers sign-known and \sigma_0-known regimes.

6.2 Status of the Architectural Claims

The MTR architecture is definitional. It specifies how research can be organized. It does not assert empirical superiority. The structural-independence predicate is operational but requires calibration before use; the pilot procedure is specified but not executed. The separation of failure events for mathematical versus empirical theses is a design requirement, not a result. The mixture over scenarios with a pre-declared prior is essential to avoid vacuous independence for mathematical theses.

6.3 Status of the Comparative Claim

The superiority of MTR over sequential research is an untested hypothesis. The protocol of Section 5 is specified but not executed. The comparison cannot be claimed until the pre-registered statistical decision rules fire. No experiments, physical tests, or code training were conducted.

6.4 Relation to Existing Systems

Co-Scientist and BioDisco implement structured multi-agent hypothesis generation and refinement. MTR’s potential distinguishing contributions—formal structural independence, explicit negative-knowledge accounting, controlled comparative protocol, and the separation of mathematical and empirical failure events—are the right candidates, but each requires definition, test, and comparison before being claimed as a contribution. The correlated-LLM-error literature warns that agreement among generative agents is not independent corroboration; shared training data and failure modes mean that consensus measures shared training, not truth.

6.5 Open Problems

1. Calibration of the structural-independence predicate. The threshold \epsilon, the prior p(\theta\mid s), and the scenario prior \pi(s) must be calibrated on a pilot before the predicate can be used.
2. General identification-set characterization. The group-orbit theorem covers the knowledge regimes listed; a general characterization of \mathcal{I}_b(\mathcal{P}) as a function of the information content of \Pi_{\exp} for arbitrary policy classes remains open.
3. Non-Gaussian noise. The scaling symmetry relies on centered Gaussian structure. Extension to scale-invariant non-Gaussian noise requires additional work.
4. Shared-generator dependence. The N-version programming and correlated-LLM-error literatures show that disjoint assumptions are not sufficient for independence. A formal account of shared generators is needed.
5. Bibliographic comparison. A full comparison of the packaging with the existing proximal causal learning and system identification literature is required before any novelty claim.

---

7. Conclusion

This work has developed the MTR architecture as a definitional framework for portfolio-based scientific research, and has applied it to a formal problem in proxy-control identifiability. The mathematical core establishes: in the linear Gaussian state-space class with free process-noise variance, the proxy coefficient b is not merely sign-ambiguous but entirely unidentifiable when c is unknown; fixing the process-noise variance collapses the obstruction to a \mathbb{Z}/2 sign symmetry; and fixing the observation coefficient c—whether or not \sigma_\eta is fixed—breaks the entire scaling symmetry and makes b identifiable under the excitation condition U_0\neq 0, U_1=0. The identification set is characterized by the group-orbit theorem \mathcal{I}_b = G_K\cdot b^\star, with seven cases for generic parameters and separate treatment of the a=0 degeneracy and the \sigma_0-dependence.

The mathematical results are classical. The contribution is the explicit packaging for proxy control, with policy-class dependence made explicit, the a=0 degeneracy catalogued, and the group-orbit structure identified. The structural-independence predicate is defined but requires calibration, and its extension to mathematical theses requires a mixture-based failure-event formalization. The comparative claim—that MTR outperforms sequential research—is an untested hypothesis. No experiments, physical tests, or code training were conducted.

The value of the work at this stage is that it states, without inflation, what is classical, what is packaging, what is operational, and what is untested. Until the dossiers are complete and the protocol is frozen and timestamped, no further claim is warranted.


