Appendix A: Mathematical Background and Supporting Results

A.1 The Innovation Form and Observational Equivalence

Definition A.1 (Linear Gaussian state-space class). The class \mathcal{M}_{\text{lin}} consists of models

X_{t+1} = aX_t + bU_t + \eta_t, \qquad Y_t = cX_t + dU_t + \epsilon_t,

with a,b,c,d\in\mathbb{R}, \eta_t\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_\eta^2), \epsilon_t\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_\epsilon^2), X_0\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_0^2), and \sigma_\eta,\sigma_\epsilon,\sigma_0>0.

Definition A.2 (Observational equivalence). For a policy class \Pi,

M \sim_\Pi M' \iff \forall \pi\in\Pi,\ P_M^\pi(Y_{0:T},U_{0:T}) = P_{M'}^\pi(Y_{0:T},U_{0:T}).

Definition A.3 (Identification set). For a functional \tau and observed laws \mathcal{P} = \{P^\pi : \pi\in\Pi_{\exp}\},

\mathcal{I}_\tau(\mathcal{P}) = \{\tau(M) : M\in\mathcal{M},\ P_M^\pi = P^\pi\ \forall \pi\in\Pi_{\exp}\}.

The identification set is indexed by the policy class actually experimented, \Pi_{\exp}, not merely by the knowledge regime. This distinction is central to the analysis: a model parameter may be identifiable under one experimental design and unidentifiable under another.

Remark A.1. The innovation form of a linear state-space model separates the deterministic input–output transfer function from the noise transfer function:

y(t) = G(q;\theta)u(t) + H(q;\theta)e(t),

G(q;\theta) = C(\theta)(qI - A(\theta))^{-1}B(\theta),
\qquad
H(q;\theta) = C(\theta)(qI - A(\theta))^{-1}K(\theta) + I,

where K(\theta) is the Kalman gain. For the scalar system considered here, A=a, B=b, C=c, and

G(q) = \frac{cb}{q-a} + d.

The output noise spectrum is

S_Y(\omega) = \left|\frac{c}{e^{i\omega}-a}\right|^2\sigma_\eta^2 + \sigma_\epsilon^2.

Under rich excitation, observational equivalence is equality of G(q) and S_Y(\omega).

Lemma A.1 (Steady-state Kalman gain). For the scalar system, the steady-state Kalman gain satisfies the discrete algebraic Riccati equation (DARE)

P = a^2P + \sigma_\eta^2 - \frac{a^2c^2P^2}{c^2P+\sigma_\epsilon^2},
\qquad
K = \frac{acP}{c^2P+\sigma_\epsilon^2}.

Proof. The DARE for the scalar case follows from the general discrete-time algebraic Riccati equation. The steady-state gain is obtained by solving the ARE for P and computing L = PC^\top(CPC^\top+R)^{-1}. \square

Remark A.2. The output spectrum S_Y(\omega) already carries all identification-relevant information; the gain K is not needed for the identification set and is omitted from the main analysis.

A.2 Persistent Excitation

Definition A.4 (Persistent excitation). A discrete-time signal u(t) is said to be persistently exciting of order n if the following limit exists:

R_u(\tau) = \lim_{N\to\infty}\frac{1}{N}\sum_{t=1}^{N}u(t)u(t-\tau),

and the matrix

\begin{bmatrix}
R_u(0) & R_u(1) & \cdots & R_u(n-1) \\
R_u(-1) & R_u(0) & & \vdots \\
\vdots & & \ddots & R_u(1) \\
R_u(1-n) & \cdots & R_u(1) & R_u(0)
\end{bmatrix}

is nonsingular.

Theorem A.1 (Persistent excitation order for linear process identification). The identifiability of an nth-order linear process

G(q) = \frac{b_1q^{-1}+\cdots+b_nq^{-n}}{1+a_1q^{-1}+\cdots+a_nq^{-n}}

requires the test signal to be persistently exciting of order 2n. This statement holds for both open-loop and closed-loop tests. The frequency-domain interpretation is that the spectrum of the signal is nonzero in at least n frequencies in the interval (-\pi,\pi). A pseudorandom binary sequence (PRBS) of length M is persistently exciting of order M; a step function is persistently exciting of order one only.

Proof. The result is classical and follows from the need to invert the n\times n covariance matrix in the least-squares estimation of the finite impulse response model. \square

Proposition A.1 (Direct excitation condition for cb). Let the input sequence satisfy U_0\neq 0 and U_1=0. Then

\mathbb{E}[Y_1] = cbU_0,

so the product cb is identified directly.

Proof. From the state equation, X_1 = aX_0 + bU_0 + \eta_0. Since X_0\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_0^2) and \eta_0\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_\eta^2) are centered, \mathbb{E}[X_1] = bU_0. From the observation equation, Y_1 = cX_1 + dU_1 + \epsilon_1. With U_1=0, \mathbb{E}[Y_1] = c\mathbb{E}[X_1] = cbU_0. \square

This condition is sufficient for identifying cb and is adopted as Assumption 3.1 in the main text.

A.3 Discrete and Continuous Symmetries in Identifiability

Definition A.5 (SLING). A parameter is structurally locally identifiable but not globally (SLING) if, for almost all parameter vectors, there is a neighbourhood in which the parameter is uniquely determined, but there exist finitely many different parameter values that produce the same output.

Theorem A.2 (Discrete symmetries and SLING). Discrete symmetries are the origin of parameters which are structurally locally identifiable but not globally. If a parameter is SLING, then for a given output, finitely many different values are possible for this parameter. The existence of discrete symmetries in a model yields a more precise structural identifiability and observability analysis, which can distinguish between SLING and globally identifiable parameters.

Proof sketch. The proof proceeds by characterizing the discrete symmetry group of the model and showing that the orbit of a parameter under this group is finite. The parameter is then locally identifiable but not globally identifiable, with the orbit providing the finite set of observationally equivalent values. \square

Theorem A.3 (Continuous symmetries and non-identifiability). The existence of continuous symmetries leaving the output invariant represents an obstruction to local observability and local identifiability. Scaling symmetries are a particular type of continuous symmetry detected by the Scaling Invariance Method (SIM). However, SIM considers scaling symmetries as the only possible cause of non-identifiability and non-observability; other types of symmetries can cause the same problems without being detected by SIM.

Proof sketch. A continuous symmetry generates a one-parameter family of transformations that leave the output invariant. The orbit of a parameter under this family is infinite, so the parameter cannot be distinguished from infinitely many other values. \square

Remark A.3. In the linear Gaussian class, both types of symmetry coexist when \sigma_\eta is free, and the continuous symmetry dominates. The collapse of S_\lambda to \mathbb{Z}/2 under fixed \sigma_\eta is precisely the passage from continuous to discrete obstruction.

