Appendix B: Proofs of the Scaling Symmetry and Identification Set

B.1 Proof of Proposition 2.1 (Scaling Symmetry)

Proposition B.1. In \mathcal{M}_{\text{lin}} with centered Gaussian noises and initial condition, for every \lambda\in\mathbb{R}^\ast the map

S_\lambda : (a,b,c,d,\sigma_\eta,\sigma_\epsilon,\sigma_0) \mapsto 
(a,\lambda b,c/\lambda,d,|\lambda|\sigma_\eta,\sigma_\epsilon,|\lambda|\sigma_0)

is an automorphism of \mathcal{M}_{\text{lin}} preserving P_M^\pi(Y_{0:T},U_{0:T}) for every \pi\in\Pi_{\text{closed}}. The family \{S_\lambda\} is a one-parameter group isomorphic to (\mathbb{R}^\ast,\cdot).

Proof. Fix \lambda\neq 0 and define X'_t = \lambda X_t, \eta'_t = \lambda\eta_t. Then \eta'_t\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\lambda^2\sigma_\eta^2), so \sigma'_\eta = |\lambda|\sigma_\eta. The state equation becomes

X'_{t+1} = \lambda X_{t+1} = \lambda(aX_t + bU_t + \eta_t) = a(\lambda X_t) + (\lambda b)U_t + \lambda\eta_t = aX'_t + (\lambda b)U_t + \eta'_t,

so b' = \lambda b and a is unchanged. The observation equation becomes

Y'_t = cX_t + dU_t + \epsilon_t = (c/\lambda)(\lambda X_t) + dU_t + \epsilon_t = (c/\lambda)X'_t + dU_t + \epsilon_t = Y_t,

so c' = c/\lambda. The initial condition transforms as X'_0 = \lambda X_0 \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\lambda^2\sigma_0^2), giving \sigma'_0 = |\lambda|\sigma_0. For any \pi_t(Y_{0:t-1},\xi_t), observation histories coincide in law, hence interventions coincide in law, hence the joint law is preserved by induction on t. The group property S_\lambda\circ S_\mu = S_{\lambda\mu} is immediate. \square

Corollary B.1. The discrete sign-flip \Theta = S_{-1} sends (a,b,c,d,\sigma_\eta,\sigma_\epsilon,\sigma_0)\mapsto(a,-b,-c,d,\sigma_\eta,\sigma_\epsilon,\sigma_0).

B.2 Proof of Proposition 3.1 (Identification Set)

Proposition B.2. Under Assumptions 3.1 and 3.2, the identification set \mathcal{I}_b(\mathcal{P}) is characterized by the seven cases in Table 3.1.

Proof.

(i) c=0. With c=0, Y_t = dU_t + \epsilon_t. The parameter b does not enter the law. Any b' produces the same law, so \mathcal{I}_b = \mathbb{R}.

(ii) c unknown, \sigma_\eta free, b^\star\neq 0, c^\star\neq 0, a^\star\neq 0. Proposition B.1 gives S_{b'/b^\star}(M^\star) observationally equivalent with target b' for every b'\neq 0. Since cb is identifiable and nonzero under Assumption 3.1, b'=0 is excluded. Hence \mathcal{I}_b = \mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}.

(iii) c unknown, \sigma_\eta fixed, b^\star\neq 0, c^\star\neq 0, a^\star\neq 0. With \sigma_\eta fixed to \bar\sigma_\eta, the scaling symmetry S_\lambda requires |\lambda|\bar\sigma_\eta = \bar\sigma_\eta, so |\lambda|=1. The residual group is \{S_1,S_{-1}\}\cong\mathbb{Z}/2. The orbit of b^\star is \{-b^\star,b^\star\}.

(iv)–(v) c=c_0\neq 0 known. The requirement c_0/\lambda = c_0 forces \lambda=1. The symmetry group is trivial. Proposition B.3 below gives b = g_1/c_0 under the excitation condition U_0\neq 0, U_1=0.

(vi) b^\star=0, c^\star\neq 0, a^\star\neq 0. With b^\star=0, the state is autonomous AR(1) and Y_t inherits the AR(1) autocorrelation of X. A candidate with b'\neq 0 introduces a policy-dependent component detectable under Assumption 3.1. If c'=0, the AR component disappears, contradicting c^\star\neq 0. Hence b'=0, so \mathcal{I}_b = \{0\}.

(vii) b^\star=0, c^\star=0. With c=0, Y_t = dU_t + \epsilon_t. Any M' with c'=0 produces the same law regardless of b'. Hence \mathcal{I}_b = \mathbb{R}. \square

B.3 Proof of Proposition 3.2 (a=0 Degeneracy)

Proposition B.3. Let a^\star=0, b^\star\neq 0, c^\star\neq 0, \sigma_\eta fixed to \bar\sigma_\eta, and let V^\star = c^{\star 2}\bar\sigma_\eta^2 + \sigma_\epsilon^{\star 2} denote the true total noise variance. Under Assumption 3.1, if \sigma_0 is free, the identification set is

\mathcal{I}_b(\mathcal{P}) = \left\{ b'\in\mathbb{R} : |b'| > \frac{|c^\star b^\star|\bar\sigma_\eta}{\sqrt{V^\star}} \right\}.

If \sigma_0 is known and \sigma_0\neq\sigma_\eta, then the set collapses to \{\pm b^\star\}.

Proof. With a=0,

X_t = bU_{t-1} + \eta_{t-1}, \qquad Y_t = dU_t + cbU_{t-1} + c\eta_{t-1} + \epsilon_t.

The noise term c\eta_{t-1} + \epsilon_t is white, so the total noise variance

V = c^2\bar\sigma_\eta^2 + \sigma_\epsilon^2

is identifiable, but c^2\bar\sigma_\eta^2 and \sigma_\epsilon^2 are not separately. The input–output law identifies d, cb, and V.

For any b' with |b'| > |c^\star b^\star|\bar\sigma_\eta/\sqrt{V^\star}, set c' = c^\star b^\star/b' and \sigma_\epsilon'^2 = V - c'^2\bar\sigma_\eta^2. Then c'b' = c^\star b^\star and c'^2\bar\sigma_\eta^2 + \sigma_\epsilon'^2 = V, so the law is reproduced. The strict inequality follows from \sigma_\epsilon > 0. If \sigma_0 is known and \sigma_0\neq\sigma_\eta, the difference

V_0 - V = c^2(\sigma_0^2 - \sigma_\eta^2)

pins |c| and the set collapses to \{\pm b^\star\}. \square

Corollary B.2 (a=0, case (vi)). If b^\star=0, c^\star\neq 0, a^\star=0, and c is unknown, then \mathcal{I}_b = \mathbb{R} when \sigma_0=\sigma_\eta: the model with c'=0 and adjusted \sigma_\epsilon' reproduces the white output law for any b'. If \sigma_0\neq\sigma_\eta, then \mathcal{I}_b = \{0\}.

B.4 Proof of Proposition 3.3 (Conditional Identifiability Under Known c)

Proposition B.4. Let \mathcal{M}_{\text{lin}}^{c_0} be \mathcal{M}_{\text{lin}} with c=c_0\in\mathbb{R}^\ast fixed and known. Let \Pi_{\text{exc}}\subseteq\Pi_{\text{open}} consist of policies whose induced inputs satisfy U_0\neq 0 and U_1=0. Then b is identifiable in \mathcal{M}_{\text{lin}}^{c_0} under \Pi_{\text{exc}}, regardless of whether \sigma_\eta is free or fixed.

Proof. With U_0\neq 0 and U_1=0, Proposition A.1 gives \mathbb{E}[Y_1] = c_0 b U_0, so g_1 = c_0 b is identifiable. Since c_0\neq 0 is known, b = g_1/c_0. The scaling symmetry S_\lambda sends c_0 to c_0/\lambda; the requirement c_0/\lambda = c_0 forces \lambda=1, so S_\lambda is broken entirely. \square

B.5 Proof of Proposition 3.4 (Excitation Does Not Break Symmetry)

Proposition B.5. Let \Pi\subseteq\Pi_{\text{open}}. For every \pi\in\Pi and every \lambda\in\mathbb{R}^\ast, M\sim_\Pi S_\lambda(M). Consequently, excitation alone cannot break S_\lambda, and b is not identifiable in \mathcal{M}_{\text{lin}} under any open-loop policy when c is unknown.

Proof. Immediate from Proposition B.1, which holds for arbitrary \Pi\subseteq\Pi_{\text{closed}} and a fortiori for \Pi\subseteq\Pi_{\text{open}}. \square

