Appendix C: Group-Orbit Theorem and Completeness

C.1 Residual Symmetry Groups

Definition C.1 (Residual symmetry group). Let K denote a knowledge regime, i.e., a specification of which parameters are known and which are free. The residual symmetry group G_K is the subgroup of \{S_\lambda : \lambda\in\mathbb{R}^\ast\} that preserves the known parameters.

Theorem C.1 (Group-orbit theorem). The identification set of b is the orbit of b^\star under the residual symmetry group:

\mathcal{I}_b = G_K \cdot b^\star.

The residual groups are:

· G = \mathbb{R}^\ast when c is unknown and \sigma_\eta is free.
· G = \mathbb{Z}/2 when c is unknown and \sigma_\eta is fixed.
· G = \{1\} when c is known.
· G = \mathbb{Z}/2 with sign constraint when \mathrm{sgn}(c) is known.

Proof. The orbit of b^\star under S_\lambda is \{\lambda b^\star : \lambda\in G_K\}. The constraint that the known parameters be preserved determines G_K. The identification set is precisely this orbit because any model observationally equivalent to M^\star must lie in the orbit of M^\star under the symmetry group, and the target b evaluates to the corresponding orbit element. \square

C.2 Completeness Under Generic Parameters

Theorem C.2 (Completeness). With a^\star\neq 0 and Assumption 3.1, the seven cases of Table 3.1 plus the a=0 degeneracies of Proposition B.3 partition the generic parameter space by knowledge regime: whether c is known or unknown, whether \sigma_\eta is free or fixed, and whether b^\star=0.

Proof sketch. The cases are exhaustive over the knowledge-regime dimensions. The a=0 degeneracy is treated separately. Sign-known and \sigma_0-known regimes are covered by the group-orbit theorem: knowing \mathrm{sgn}(c) restricts G_K to the subgroup of \mathbb{R}^\ast with positive elements; knowing \sigma_0 adds the invariant V_0 = c^2\sigma_0^2 + \sigma_\epsilon^2, which pins |c| when \sigma_0\neq\sigma_\eta. \square

