Appendix E: Prior Art and Methodological Context

E.1 The Method of Multiple Working Hypotheses

Chamberlin (1890) introduced the method of multiple working hypotheses, warning that scientists "fall in love" with their favourite hypothesis, leading to the unfortunate practice of trying to fit all evidence into a single explanation instead of finding genuine explanations for the phenomenon they study. The solution, according to Chamberlin, is to cultivate the habit of developing and comparing alternative hypotheses to explain any phenomenon observed. Platt (1964) built on this with "strong inference," suggesting that scientific progress is promoted by the consistent application of strong inference: devising and testing multiple hypotheses with crucial experiments and re-evaluating them to refine the possible explanations for a given phenomenon. Platt pointed out that multiple hypothesis testing was responsible for the fast growth of molecular biology in the 1960s, demonstrating its utility for any field that seeks rapid scientific progress.

Despite the classical pedigree, the method of multiple hypotheses is rarely used in practice. In a sample of the ecology and evolution literature, only 21 of 100 randomly selected studies tested two hypotheses, and only eight tested more than two hypotheses. This gap between theory and practice motivates MTR's formalization of portfolio-based research.

E.2 N-Version Programming and Correlated Failures

The N-version programming literature provides a cautionary precedent for MTR's structural-independence predicate. N-version programming has been proposed as a method of incorporating fault tolerance into software by independently preparing multiple versions of a program and voting on the results. This method depends for its reliability improvement on the assumption that programs that have been developed independently will fail independently.

A large-scale experiment with 27 versions of a program prepared independently from the same specification revealed that the versions were individually extremely reliable, but the number of input cases in which more than one failed was substantially more than would be expected if they were statistically independent. Minor differences in the software development environment would not have a major impact in reducing the incidence of faults that cause correlated failures. The conclusion is that independence of failure patterns in separately produced software versions cannot in general be assumed to be true, and that analysis of the reliability of N-version programming must include the effect of dependent errors.

This precedent directly undermines any claim that "multiple theses generated by different agents" constitute independent evidence. MTR's structural-independence predicate must explicitly account for shared generators, shared methods, and shared assumptions.

E.3 Correlated Errors in Large Language Models

Recent empirical work across more than 350 large language models found substantial correlation in model errors. On one leaderboard dataset, models agree 60% of the time when both models err. Crucially, larger and more accurate models have highly correlated errors, even with distinct architectures and providers. Models from the same company tend to be more correlated. The Synthetic Consensus Cascade framework shows that when pairwise error correlation remains positive, increasing the number of agents does not drive majority-error probability to zero; instead, risk approaches a non-zero correlation-dependent floor. In a stress test with individual error probability p=0.08, nine agents, and error correlation \rho=0.25, the majority-wrong probability is 3.56\%, compared with 0.031\% under independence—a 113.6-fold difference.

The implication for MTR is direct: agreement among generative agents is not independent corroboration. Shared training data and shared failure modes mean that consensus measures shared training, not truth. MTR's structural-independence predicate must therefore be calibrated against correlated-failure models, and the verification rule must exclude generative-agent confirmation from counting as verification.

E.4 Proximal Causal Learning

Tchetgen Tchetgen et al. (2020) introduce a formal potential outcome framework for proximal causal learning, which acknowledges covariate measurements as imperfect proxies of confounding mechanisms and offers an opportunity to learn about causal effects in settings where exchangeability on the basis of measured covariates fails. Sufficient conditions for nonparametric identification are given, leading to the proximal g-formula and corresponding proximal g-computation algorithm for estimation. The present work is a dynamical-systems specialization of that broader program: the "proxy" is a control input, the "confounder" is an unobserved state, and the identification question concerns the coefficient through which the control enters the state.

E.5 Structural Identifiability and Symmetries

The distinction between continuous and discrete symmetries governs the type of non-identifiability. Discrete symmetries are the origin of parameters which are structurally locally identifiable, but not globally (SLING). If a parameter is SLING, then for a given output, finitely many different values are possible for this parameter. Continuous symmetries, in contrast, represent an obstruction to local identifiability. The Scaling Invariance Method (SIM) considers scaling symmetries as the only possible cause of non-identifiability and non-observability, but other types of symmetries can cause the same problems without being detected by SIM.

For the linear Gaussian class considered here, both types coexist when \sigma_\eta is free, and the continuous symmetry dominates. The collapse of S_\lambda to \mathbb{Z}/2 under fixed \sigma_\eta is precisely the passage from continuous to discrete obstruction.

